data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR is a genetic engineering technique, based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system, that can modify the genomes of living organisms [7].\n- A synthetic guide RNA directs the Cas9 nuclease to a desired genome location, where the DNA is cut so genes can be removed or new genes added [7].\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to \"knock out\" a gene by breaking it. They can also supply a DNA repair template to replace a disease-causing mutation [2].\n- After Cas9 cuts the DNA, the cell repairs the break using available DNA, so a new gene can be injected to take the place of the cut DNA [3].\n- CRISPR SWAPnDROP extends genome editing to large-scale DNA transfer between bacterial species, demonstrated in the model organism *Escherichia coli* [4]."}

